body:
> As for the behaviour for export annotations that you mentioned, I understand what you mean although I don't completely agree. `@export_required` has the exact same functionality as `@export`, and the only addition is that it automatically includes the `PROPERTY_USAGE_REQUIRED` flag into the usages when the annotations are being parsed.

I explained this above and gave an example with `@export_range`.

If you believe `@export_required` should be an exclusive annotation (incompatible with other export annotations), then you should use an **export hint** (`PROPERTY_HINT_REQUIRED`) rather than an **usage flag** (`PROPERTY_USAGE_REQUIRED`). This is similar to how `@export_multiline` uses `PROPERTY_HINT_MULTILINE_TEXT` (which is also somewhat debatable). Property hints are exclusive, usage flags are not.

If you believe `@export_required` shouldn't be exclusive (for example, you want to change the appearance of a field in the editor using `@export_range`, `@export_multiline`, etc. AND make the field required), then I explained why this is conceptually bad in my opinion. Instead of:

```gdscript
@export_required var a: int
@export_range(-10, 10) @export_required var b: int
```

I think the annotation should act as a modifier (see also the discussion about `@no_storage`):

```gdscript
@export @required var a: int
@export_range(-10, 10) @required var b: int
```

I have renamed the annotation to `@required` and it now needs to be used in addition to an export annotation, e.g.
```gd
@export @required var node: Node = null
```
Also, I have removed the immediate `update_configuration_warnings` from `GDScriptInstance::set`. This means that the configuration warnings are only updated when the scene is saved. I think this is a decent compromise -- you still get the configuration warning even if a bit delayed, and the code changes to the engine are reduced.

@dalexeev What do you think about these changes?
